*** A WRONG RECORD I PROPAGATED ALL EVENING, AND CORRECTING IT MAKES THIS WORSE. ***  I asserted repeatedly -- in briefs, in commit messages and in three ledger entries -- that `fatal()` is 'byte-for-byte debug()'. MEASURED FALSE, found by the seat driving the pitlatch round:

    void debug(...) { ...; if ((quiet_mode && !ss) || v < 0) return; va_debug(...); }
    void fatal(...) {                                              va_debug(...); }

`debug()` carries an EARLY-OUT on quiet_mode and verbosity; `fatal()` has NONE. They are not the same function and never were. What I got right is that fatal() does not EXIT -- that part stands, and it was the point I was making each time.

THE CONSEQUENCE IS THE OPPOSITE OF REASSURING: a guest-drivable fatal() flood CANNOT BE SILENCED, not even with -q. The operator has no switch. So every row in this class is MORE severe than recorded, not less -- and the seat that found it noted that this STRENGTHENS the defect rather than excusing it.

Recorded here rather than only in a commit message, because a claim repeated in five places is not corrected by fixing one of them -- this project's own rule is to grep for the siblings before calling a correction done.

PANEL CONVERGENCE, and it is unusually clean on one axis and split on the other.

*** Q1 IS UNANIMOUS ACROSS ALL SEVEN SCRIPTABLE SEATS: cap in the four TICK CALLBACKS, not in DEVICE_TICK. ***  Codex gave the reason the others assumed: capping in DEVICE_TICK leaves the overflowing increment untouched and only limits what later code observes. The two positions bound different things, and only one of them bounds the counter.

Q2 SPLITS 5-1-1 and the split tracks nothing useful about seat class -- codex, kimi, deepseek and minimax all land on ONE EMULATED SECOND OF THE TIMER'S DELIVERED RATE (minimax expressing it through the device's own footbridge_effective_cycles helper); grok proposes a saturating store of 1; glm alone proposes a FIXED bound, INT_MAX/2. Not settled by count -- handed to the measuring seat.

*** AND CODEX'S PRE-INCREMENT POINT MEANS THE IN-TREE PRECEDENT THIS ROUND WAS ABOUT TO COPY HAS THE WRONG FORM. ***  Checked by opening it: dev_luna88k.c:252-256 reads
    d->pending_timer_interrupts ++;
    if (d->pending_timer_interrupts > (int)(LUNA88K_PSEUDO_TIMER_HZ)) {
        d->pending_timer_interrupts = 1;
-- increment FIRST, check second. It is SAFE THERE, because the reset to 1 keeps the counter far below INT_MAX so the increment can never overflow; but the FORM is the UB one, and copying it into footbridge would carry the shape into a device whose bound is the thing under dispute. The brief cited luna88k as 'the fix needs no invention'. It still does not -- but it needs the comparison moved, which is exactly the kind of detail that survives a copy-paste unexamined. Recorded against luna88k as well, since the form there is worth a note even though it is unreachable.

